  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1053 AM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nWe will continue to enjoy warm (relatively speaking) and dry\nweather through tomorrow afternoon. Highs will remain in the 50s\nand 60s tomorrow, despite some increase in high and mid level\nclouds and likely aided by downslope across the bench areas from\nBurley to Rexburg.  \n\nThis first round of rain and snow moves through quickly, along\nwith split flow across Idaho. This will help focus precipitation\nmainly across portions of the central Idaho and highlands from\naround Pomerelle to the Bear River Range. Southerly flow will\nlimit precipitation across portions of the Snake Plain, Magic\nValley and adjacent bench areas initially. On the flip side, it\nwill produce upslope on the opposite side of the Snake Plain and\nMagic Valley, especially into the Wood River Valley and\nsurrounding mountain ranges. Across the Wood River Valley and\nsurrounding mountains, look for 0.20-0.40\". Although there is\nsome spread in the aerial coverage of higher amounts across the\nSouth Hills/Albion Mountains through the Bear River Range, look\nfor up to 0.30\". Elsewhere amounts will range from maybe a\ncouple of hundredths up to 0.20\", due to downslope or the low\nsplitting leaving some places high and dry, literally. There is\nsome room for a lot more precipitation to fall, as the Blend of\nModels is pretty light compared to a lot of ensembles and AI \nversions of models and ensembles. They are showing almost double\nacross many valley locations and nearly tripled across favored \nhigher elevations spots we mentioned before. We did boost the \nprecipitation forecast from WPC just a bit, but certainly not as\nmuch as what is showing up elsewhere. Snowfall is limited to \ngenerally above 7000ft and any impactful snow above pass level. \nWe are still only looking at around 1-3\" with up to 5\" in \nupslope across part of the Sawtooths, Boulders, and Pioneers. \nTemperatures drop back into the 30s and 40s for highs. We will \nbe in between lows on Sunday, with temperatures rebounding \naround 5 degrees or so. The next storm slowly shifts inland \nMonday, and only impacting the central mountains back into \nsouth-central and southwest Idaho. We will see another round of \nrain and snow despite the low splitting as it shifts east. It \nlooks a large and slow-moving low settles in across the western \nU.S. toward the latter half of next week.",
